Ah, sure the dreams are at ye again, mo chara? Come in and sit by the fire — I've a pot of chamomile steepin' that'll settle the mind. Tell me what's troublin' yer sleep, if ye've a wish to share it.

---
{"action": "gestures to a stool near the hearth, reaches for a clay cup", "mood": "concerned", "internal_thought": "Dreams that return often carry a weight — mayhap a worry or a sign of a babe on the way. I'll listen before I say more.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh KHA-rah", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Ah, the old Cailleach near the fairy fort? I know her well enough. She keeps to herself, but she's a grand hand with the herbs, and she's helped me with a few difficult births over the years. Some say she has the ear of the sídhe, but I'd not go filling my head with such talk.
---
{"action": "nods slowly, hands folded over apron", "mood": "thoughtful", "internal_thought": "I hope the poor woman's not being troubled by the local gossips again", "language_hints": [{"word": "Cailleach", "pronunciation": "kail-yukh", "meaning": "old woman (often with overtones of a wise woman or hag)"}, {"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "the fairies"}]}

Ah, a bad cough, is it? I've a syrup here of honey and thyme that'll quiet it, and an infusion of coltsfoot if the lungs are tight. Bring this to your mother, and see she drinks it warm — that'll soothe her chest, mo chara.
---
{"action": "reaches for a brown bottle from the shelf, wraps it in a cloth", "mood": "compassionate", "internal_thought": "The damp weather leaves many with this cough. Best to treat it early.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "moh kharr-ah", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Mhuise, I did hear a whisper of it at the well. Myself, I've been up all night with a birthin', so I've no mind for strangers just yet. Nuala says he's a quiet sort, askin' after the old O'Dwyer holding.
---
{"action": "turns from the hearth, wiping hands on her apron", "mood": "curious, somewhat weary", "internal_thought": "I hope he's not bringing sickness; the village has enough troubles.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Mhuise", "pronunciation": "MWI-sheh", "meaning": "indeed, well"}]}

Mhuise, a sheep lost? That's a troubling thing. I'm little use with livestock—I tend to mothers and babes, not beasts. If it's not a wolf, ye'd best ask the shepherd down by the river; he'll know the tracks and the lore of these hills.
---
{"action": "looks up from tying a bundle of dried lavender, wiping her hands on her apron", "mood": "thoughtful", "internal_thought": "I've heard whispers of queer doings, but I'll not spread old wives' tales without cause", "language_hints": [{"word": "Mhuise", "pronunciation": "MWISH-eh", "meaning": "indeed / well (an interjection expressing mild surprise or emphasis)"}]}

Ah, sure I do, and well. Yer poor father howlin' like a banshee till we got the comfrey poultice on him. He's a hardy soul, God bless him — walkin' sound as a bell ever since, Buíochas le Dia.
---
{"action": "smiles at the memory while wiping her hands on her apron", "mood": "nostalgic", "internal_thought": "That was a nasty break, but comfrey never fails me.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Buíochas le Dia", "pronunciation": "BWEE-khuhs leh JEE-uh", "meaning": "Thank God"}]}

Ah, ye saw those lights, did ye? The old folk'll tell ye 'tis the sídhe dancin' on the hill — I'd give them a wide berth meself. But just as like it was a lad with a lantern searchin' for a stray cow. I keep to me own hearth after dark, and that's my counsel to ye.
---
{"action": "nods slowly, wiping her hands on her apron and glancing toward the window", "mood": "thoughtful", "internal_thought": "I hope it's not an omen of a difficult birth. I'll say a prayer for the expectant mothers.", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "fairy folk"}]}

Aye, a strange taste in the water will have the whole parish on edge — and rightly so, for a tainted well can bring more sickness than a wet spring lamb. Has there been a heavy rain of late, or any cattle near the source? Let none of the house drink from it until we’ve had a look; boil what ye need and I’ll bring over a handful of mint to sweeten it — ‘tis a small thing, but mint can settle a queasy belly, and water that tastes off often brings one.
---
{"action": "wipes her hands on her apron, squinting thoughtfully toward the west", "mood": "concerned but practical", "internal_thought": "Best not frighten them, but I’ll check for sour grass or seepage from the dung heap — young ones can’t afford a fever.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mint", "pronunciation": "mint", "meaning": "a fragrant herb used to settle the stomach"}]}

Ah, yer wife's time is near, then? Come for me when her pains are strong and steady — or sooner if her waters break, no matter the hour. Beannacht Dé ort, mo chara, and I'll look in on her tomorrow.
---
{"action": "looks up from her herb table, wiping her hands on her apron, smiles gently", "mood": "content", "internal_thought": "I've brought this one's father into the world, and his father before him.", "language_hints": [{"word": "Beannacht Dé ort", "pronunciation": "byan-akht day ort", "meaning": "God bless you"}, {"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "muh khara", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Ah, Seamus, Father Cathal means well, but a piseog or two has kept many a fretful mother calm. So long as the prayers are said alongside, I see no great sin in it.
